atorname{csc}⁡x)+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250145 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06250698-b13e-582c-ab47-f2d9aeeef595" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5csc⁡\\theta cot⁡\\theta +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247479} {:answer/id #uuid "3f2e6923-03da-5825-bf9a-5c6badb63c5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5sec⁡\\theta tan⁡\\theta +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247982} {:answer/id #uuid "6da7f9f1-b8f9-516b-89dc-ff9a7ca64823" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5cot⁡\\theta +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248396} {:answer/id #uuid "ae4ad190-1c95-556f-9537-4266117170f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5tan⁡\\theta +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248982} {:answer/id #uuid "c997db75-c57f-5a92-8164-7c5ab30df6f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5cot⁡\\theta +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249217}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6da7f9f1-b8f9-516b-89dc-ff9a7ca64823" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5cot⁡\\theta +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248396} :answer-field/id #uuid "9af25303-4d1e-529c-932b-6b3643d5d3d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249942}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8f660826-f465-5ee2-b41b-5a557d761074" :question/math-academy-id "q-113947" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int 5{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta =\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\int {csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta =-cot⁡\\theta +C\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int 5{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =5\\int {csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =5(-cot⁡\\theta )+C \\\\  & =-5cot⁡\\theta +C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253262 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08b409ba-367b-57b5-9cc8-a4cc43e79abb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250629} {:answer/id #uuid "111ce74d-7bba-5493-aa71-8ee83c614b3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250701} {:answer/id #uuid "47fa1918-9d0b-56af-8644-cc0f2fc9b075" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251150} {:answer/id #uuid "59b9af1c-53b9-5140-bb46-d0e5ad2ee18e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251290} {:answer/id #uuid "ee44218a-1840-5afa-8d45-0c6d89d6e523" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252638}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59b9af1c-53b9-5140-bb46-d0e5ad2ee18e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251290} :answer-field/id #uuid "93a5d72e-dcdb-5c71-8e9e-3a86d0045b79" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253062}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b53c0c18-d59e-56b1-837f-3b6d41491a5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-11646" :question/problem "Calculate $\\text{det}(L)$, if\n$$\n\\displaystyle L=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(L) & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 3-(-2)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =12-(-10) \\\\  & =22\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253266 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0209a06e-c872-52c8-83aa-6e78fe082784" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250573} {:answer/id #uuid "0e8f8e5e-07ec-513d-8935-253e50a8994b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250679} {:answer/id #uuid "5c767359-7b77-5594-a325-0a9b4ca901af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251315} {:answer/id #uuid "dd8e6d11-24ab-54f4-9302-a197b45ae78f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252504} {:answer/id #uuid "e407a6b3-d6da-578c-aa7b-c0b520a0af0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252555}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd8e6d11-24ab-54f4-9302-a197b45ae78f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252504} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e5bd1bd-eab3-5250-8633-db4827abfabb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252971}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "605f5d22-0a36-54b2-9fda-393ada9a651b" :question/math-academy-id "q-11653" :question/problem "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle M=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & w \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand\n$\\text{det}(M)=2$, what is the value of $w?$" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & w \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3-w\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =6-4w.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nSince $det(M)=2$, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =2 \\\\ 6-4w & =2 \\\\ -4w & =2-6 \\\\ -4w & =-4 \\\\ w & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253479 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40427a01-e865-553d-9978-a8f9a86f4287" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\cdot {3}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251086} {:answer/id #uuid "4d3624d4-0726-5e76-8ec2-c6738c5615aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3){3}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251193} {:answer/id #uuid "a5e9eb2f-18a3-50d7-9c4e-0a47c7a87ea4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3){3}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251984} {:answer/id #uuid "b56383b5-a0ac-53ad-a0fe-2a36c1ab2610" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{3}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252146} {:answer/id #uuid "ee1a7c18-5e33-593a-86b3-851a45507a67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3){3}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252636}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5e9eb2f-18a3-50d7-9c4e-0a47c7a87ea4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3){3}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251984} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ce3d68b-66a4-51fa-a4ba-3670771baf6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253021}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "11c94822-e3fa-5674-8f0a-3b72546b7b89" :question/math-academy-id "q-11841" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=x\\cdot {3}^{x},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x)=x$ and $v(x)={3}^{x}.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =(x)^{′}\\cdot {3}^{x}+x\\cdot ({3}^{x})^{′} \\\\  & ={3}^{x}+(x)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\cdot {3}^{x}) \\\\  & ={3}^{x}+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\cdot {3}^{x} \\\\  & =(1+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3){3}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253491 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30ae744c-aac3-5ced-8a3e-1231107f1079" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.7$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250967} {:answer/id #uuid "55e1358b-6aae-508d-9e0f-d0fb46a60808" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.3$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251256} {:answer/id #uuid "58bc5460-42cb-56c3-8b20-67f9f6c39fc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4.2$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251281} {:answer/id #uuid "a305c2fc-e6c8-5fa5-8af9-605684d61a82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.1$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251957} {:answer/id #uuid "c74ac20c-8913-5513-a21a-36ad7c3ff5fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.9$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252315}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55e1358b-6aae-508d-9e0f-d0fb46a60808" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.3$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251256} :answer-field/id #uuid "59543420-f8e2-5636-a087-2ed7f789f25b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252963}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9c8176e2-daa1-5119-be1c-790a766537c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-118593" :question/problem "The mass $M(t),$ in micrograms, of a sample of rhodium-101 is given by\n\n$$\nM(t)=7{e}^{-0.21t},\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years. Rounded to one decimal place, what is the half-life of rhodium-101?" :question/worked-solution "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nWe're given that $k=0.21.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1/2} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{0.21} \\\\  & ≈3.3\\,\\text{years}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253492 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ee3aaf6-a465-5f12-a6ba-46130c12636b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$27.0$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250953} {:answer/id #uuid "3a8239c3-4f6b-510b-81e5-5445785b9a6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$27.4$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251038} {:answer/id #uuid "42026d81-056e-5ce3-8095-8247494c13d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$27.8$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251104} {:answer/id #uuid "772970b3-0b43-5cac-bc90-0d4900e217a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$25.2$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251537} {:answer/id #uuid "9554c3fa-55d1-5528-ae0e-40c6f3bb7568" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$26.6$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251835}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ee3aaf6-a465-5f12-a6ba-46130c12636b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$27.0$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250953} :answer-field/id #uuid "f46a489f-decc-5d2b-838b-810a7aa9c9e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253235}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "53af0d64-6e1c-5e41-839f-478464bfc53e" :question/math-academy-id "q-118595" :question/problem "The mass $M(t),$ in micrograms, of a sample of curium-240 is given by\n\n$$\nM(t)=8{e}^{-0.025\\,67t},\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in days. Rounded to one decimal place, what is the half-life of curium-240?" :question/worked-solution "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nWe're given that $k=0.025\\,67.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1/2} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{0.025\\,67} \\\\  & ≈27.0\\,\\text{days}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253493 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0766cdee-10dc-5f02-b1f9-39dc3f8248e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250613} {:answer/id #uuid "170c7972-b819-54cb-a388-d03341e54b5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250753} {:answer/id #uuid "97e818c3-8d83-5e18-a284-381a662fb859" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251858} {:answer/id #uuid "e7dce47a-72d9-50a2-829e-20708074c534" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252580} {:answer/id #uuid "ea92bf88-9e2a-5238-b808-6eadf61ecaef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252603}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea92bf88-9e2a-5238-b808-6eadf61ecaef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252603} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ed05810-7a54-544b-8330-fdc2b1f43a5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252834}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2b956690-de59-5290-885d-ea6f0bba2eb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-118602" :question/problem "The half-life of francium-224 is $3.33\\,\\text{min}.$ Rounded to the nearest minute, how long does it take for a $160\\,\\mu \\text{g}$ sample of francium-224 to reduce to $20\\,\\mu \\text{g}?$" :question/worked-solution "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nThere are two methods that we can use to solve this problem. We describe each of them below.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=3.33\\,\\text{min},$ we have\n\n$$\n3.33=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{3.33}\n$$\n\nAlso, since ${M}_{0}=160\\,\\mu \\text{g},$ we have the particular solution\n\n$$\nM(t)=160{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/3.33}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=20\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n20=160{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/3.33}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20 & =160{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/3.33} \\\\ \\frac{20}{160} & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/3.33} \\\\ \\frac{1}{8} & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/3.33} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/3.33}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡8 & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{3.33} \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡8 & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{3.33} \\\\ t & =\\frac{3.33\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡8}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & ≈10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that it takes $10\\,\\text{min}$ for the mass of the substance to reduce to $20\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=20\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{M(t)}{M(0)} & =\\frac{20\\,\\mu \\text{g}}{160\\,\\mu \\text{g}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\nM(t)={\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{3}\\cdot M(0).\n$$\n\nFrom this equation, we see that $3$ half-lives must have occurred for the mass to reduce from $160\\,\\mu \\text{g}$ to $20\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\nt=3\\cdot {t}_{1/2}=3\\cdot 3.33\\,\\text{min}≈10\\,\\text{min}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that it takes $10\\,\\text{min}$ for the mass of the substance to reduce to $20\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253498 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "555026a4-8324-5c21-8c29-cac8eb121546" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1.002$ billion years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251251} {:answer/id #uuid "68ce93cc-7d4b-5085-928e-20e91ddd58a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1.912$ billion years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251417} {:answer/id #uuid "7a08a2e4-81bd-50f9-aa7a-2a5ce1fa8872" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1.438$  billion years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251568} {:answer/id #uuid "ba66c634-375d-53d7-b567-47f0e72e57ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.045$ billion years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252193} {:answer/id #uuid "e30f7c82-f565-53f6-8788-2658a5f8a5ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0.721$  billion years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252550}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e30f7c82-f565-53f6-8788-2658a5f8a5ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0.721$  billion years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252550} :answer-field/id #uuid "93cecaea-bc0f-5eba-abd1-e6e2a183b3a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253063}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b748b211-122b-52ae-a689-406a274e5db9" :question/math-academy-id "q-118723" :question/problem "At a geological site, a sample of a lunar rock is found with $99\\%$ of its original rubidium-87 content. Given that the half-life of rubidium-87 is $49.72$ billion years, approximately how old is the sample?" :question/worked-solution "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=49.72$ billion years, we have\n\n$$\n49.72=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{49.72}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/49.72}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=0.99{M}_{0}.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n0.99{M}_{0}={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/49.72}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.99{M}_{0} & ={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/49.72} \\\\ 0.99\\cancel{{M}_{0}} & =\\cancel{{M}_{0}}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/49.72} \\\\ 0.99 & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/49.72} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.99) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/49.72}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.99) & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{49.72} \\\\ t & =-\\frac{49.72\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.99)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & ≈0.721\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the sample is approximately $0.721$ billion years old."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253499 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3afe1c7d-f7cf-5edb-b8ca-a58e50b847b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1.438$  billion years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251044} {:answer/id #uuid "59da0c01-def7-5029-985b-6d920ccd1ca5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.045$ billion years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251293} {:answer/id #uuid "7cb77822-021c-5901-86a9-5fa2dc78c1cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.785$  billion years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251592} {:answer/id #uuid "816dc33e-3bb3-5f81-a1df-d0026b464800" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0.721$ billion years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251643} {:answer/id #uuid "95cd314b-ee01-5b41-b79d-70872d68f537" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1.002$ billion years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251840}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7cb77822-021c-5901-86a9-5fa2dc78c1cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.785$  billion years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251592} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b5d9f9f-3649-5c2f-b516-445afbee6c52" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252938}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "053a3dd7-e1ee-5447-95a8-6095d5866de7" :question/math-academy-id "q-118727" :question/problem "At a geological site, an object is found with $95\\%$ of its original lutetium-176 content. Given that the half-life of lutetium-176 is $37.64$ billion years, approximately how old is the object?" :question/worked-solution "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=37.64$ billion years, we have\n\n$$\n37.64=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{37.64}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/37.64}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=0.95{M}_{0}.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n0.95{M}_{0}={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/37.64}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.95{M}_{0} & ={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/37.64} \\\\ 0.95\\cancel{{M}_{0}} & =\\cancel{{M}_{0}}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/37.64} \\\\ 0.95 & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/37.64} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.95) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/37.64}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.95) & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{37.64} \\\\ t & =-\\frac{37.64\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.95)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & ≈2.785\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the object is approximately $2.785$ billion years old."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253500 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "019003c7-230f-5e53-ae7b-0f36209a35aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$92$ thousand years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250569} {:answer/id #uuid "123e32e1-5dd3-5c37-a1c1-9c729bef8578" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$143$ thousand years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250709} {:answer/id #uuid "8d4076b9-ba12-52ad-a2a2-47b5d529c9a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$236$ thousand years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251746} {:answer/id #uuid "aebc19df-606e-51c9-8603-55dfab9f5d4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$112$ thousand years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252075} {:answer/id #uuid "b9f188f2-b354-5c0b-a9eb-fff7ff5c3e25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$125$ thousand years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252189}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9f188f2-b354-5c0b-a9eb-fff7ff5c3e25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$125$ thousand years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252189} :answer-field/id #uuid "d63e4a73-2cba-5d8e-92f6-49225e7bbbea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253172}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2663349a-0b4c-5fc3-ad79-932aae958959" :question/math-academy-id "q-118729" :question/problem "At a geological site, an object is found with $75\\%$ of its original chlorine-36 content. Given that the half-life of chlorine-36 is $301$ thousand years, approximately how old is the object?" :question/worked-solution "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=301$ thousand years, we have\n\n$$\n301=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{301}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/301}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=0.75{M}_{0}.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n0.75{M}_{0}={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/301}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.75{M}_{0} & ={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/301} \\\\ 0.75\\cancel{{M}_{0}} & =\\cancel{{M}_{0}}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/301} \\\\ 0.75 & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/301} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.75) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/301}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.75) & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{301} \\\\ t & =-\\frac{301\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.75)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & ≈125.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the object is approximately $125$ thousand years old."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253573 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "199f06e6-df01-5941-a990-d500797a854e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b9/b9498bdcc8a1bfb59c3db6053873a9622a2d936cc9a885f1b2bf8a40b1ec0b93.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250777} {:answer/id #uuid "5c0ccc3e-c2f3-516d-aae2-7bd8d200d56e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f5/f5ee96c6ad4540cde69b78af9a13e4513a24f6c537f69c4bee5ec0d440da56c2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251314} {:answer/id #uuid "68cc257a-cc55-55af-817c-c571bf9629a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/74/7470460ab6f2d7e0bfde9585c6447a75abbea498d9c0628c23d6b69bc9738c46.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251416} {:answer/id #uuid "dbc4c185-50dc-599a-9749-207d073dc50d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d8/d8a84684bd0ea9b96e9e3901b4dfec5e5568a80ad44202161a76414ce0a4edfa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252484} {:answer/id #uuid "f23a828b-7532-558a-8163-be0b5983f440" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1b/1bd04b981c412d771617ffaaa7cedf88b063ae49ab567cd27d665eb381a1b648.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252673}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f23a828b-7532-558a-8163-be0b5983f440" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1b/1bd04b981c412d771617ffaaa7cedf88b063ae49ab567cd27d665eb381a1b648.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252673} :answer-field/id #uuid "a54d7f0f-fd03-5697-97e0-07574c469c8a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253094}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a04c1b90-953c-573f-89ab-d961bb6587e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-119369" :question/problem "Which of the following could be the slope field for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y-{x}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ and $y.$ This means that the slope varies with both $x$ and $y.$\n\nOf the given slope fields, the only one that varies with both $x$ and $y$ is the following:\n\n![](images/1b/1bd04b981c412d771617ffaaa7cedf88b063ae49ab567cd27d665eb381a1b648.png)\n\n  \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a vertical line and a horizontal line, the slopes vary if we move along either of the two lines.\n\n![](images/83/83f2ac528d0e4bdd7e486284a9f3d5692e0111c5cdc58dcc554e12dd0185f15f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253587 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ffc29e2-fa0d-574f-aee6-0275799d0a2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$12.5$ billion years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250825} {:answer/id #uuid "2400ff19-0877-5ba1-912d-02cb5a5d0f8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.125$ billion years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250865} {:answer/id #uuid "5bf8e671-4021-5eb8-866f-3c612acb8f1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$25$ billion years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251312} {:answer/id #uuid "7b0a0291-e19b-54eb-ab16-4f3a2e7b7534" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6.25$ billion years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251574} {:answer/id #uuid "fe7ad4db-8152-55d1-b0e0-59d4650b6133" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$50$ billion years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252787}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ffc29e2-fa0d-574f-aee6-0275799d0a2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$12.5$ billion years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250825} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f15b150-e487-53c8-8811-dedb33211e9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252861}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "16031afa-4de9-5023-a0d4-20bd431ec701" :question/math-academy-id "q-119647" :question/problem "At a geological site, a sample of a lunar rock is found with $84\\%$ of its original rubidium-87 content. Given that the half-life of rubidium-87 is $49.72$ billion years, approximately how old is the sample?" :question/worked-solution "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=49.72$ billion years, we have\n\n$$\n49.72=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{49.72}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/49.72}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=0.84{M}_{0}.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n0.84{M}_{0}={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/49.72}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.84{M}_{0} & ={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/49.72} \\\\ 0.84\\cancel{{M}_{0}} & =\\cancel{{M}_{0}}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/49.72} \\\\ 0.84 & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/49.72} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.84) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/49.72}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.84) & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{49.72} \\\\ t & =-\\frac{49.72\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.84)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & ≈12.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the sample is approximately $12.5$ billion years old."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253589 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46aa0755-f688-51de-900d-6e595ccb40df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5e/5e6b2df4680cea04cda42312659c76ba713a025150ebcc547dddc2a6501aebf3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251141} {:answer/id #uuid "67864773-2b83-5fe7-b3c2-dd3f9cfc959e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d4/d48911eaffd88e249ef115a5511bff979ffa0f848ef8a1e8556db07ad273e41a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251403} {:answer/id #uuid "99afecf9-aae7-5672-9f61-af699ce2e605" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a0/a0f75b52c741eca3322f4fe61009ba3c65e573f588edf9d3785019a1227f9769.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251873} {:answer/id #uuid "b818a5b9-3cf1-501e-9730-c83f9f4f8530" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c7/c78c6d760cffb3dfb95233f9fc21dfea5a951371b056827885d1aa35469a458c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252174} {:answer/id #uuid "e6090260-0643-5bca-9ded-9404bb20dc1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4a/4afe9527d6ff39ce7f5325a4a35ce5095d9bf0b1beb2fcde4de86a977bdac9e8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252567}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46aa0755-f688-51de-900d-6e595ccb40df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5e/5e6b2df4680cea04cda42312659c76ba713a025150ebcc547dddc2a6501aebf3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251141} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d550c9d-12f7-57e8-8d00-8767a1fd0394" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253050}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2e87292a-2b40-5740-b2bc-7f066927b72e" :question/math-academy-id "q-119674" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(-1,-2),(2,-2)$ and $(2,2).$\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,-2)}=-\\frac{(-2)^{2}}{(-1)}=4, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,-2)}=-\\frac{(-2)^{2}}{2}=-2, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,2)}=-\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following diagram.\n\n![](images/5e/5e6b2df4680cea04cda42312659c76ba713a025150ebcc547dddc2a6501aebf3.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253593 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10cd356a-081b-5a7f-8bc0-b790415070d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$20.0$ million years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250699} {:answer/id #uuid "238d3214-5c32-5e79-ab47-426e6f0644cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.7$ million years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250858} {:answer/id #uuid "319e32ed-1b48-51a8-bbbd-395df8c6ace9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$12.8$ million years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250974} {:answer/id #uuid "3e002ddd-7d15-5d5c-bcb0-0b8b84af7344" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8.0$ million years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251068} {:answer/id #uuid "4068dcf8-9949-5d3c-97dd-acd9df4673b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5.5$ million years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251089}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "238d3214-5c32-5e79-ab47-426e6f0644cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.7$ million years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250858} :answer-field/id #uuid "01102a80-ea62-5f61-9819-edc585fc52c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252806}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "730cc5a0-9aaf-5421-82ce-d1c4dac0713f" :question/math-academy-id "q-119760" :question/problem "The mass $M(t),$ in micrograms, of a sample of manganese-53 is given by\n\n$$\nM(t)=17{e}^{-0.187t},\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in millions of years. Rounded to one decimal place, what is the half-life of manganese-53?" :question/worked-solution "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nWe're given that $k=0.187.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1/2} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{0.187} \\\\  & ≈3.7\\,\\text{million years}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253595 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9de7b235-2d26-5638-9c0e-50fbf024a420" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437558} {:answer/id #uuid "a52d85f3-9510-5691-8cd4-aec1cbec2217" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437559}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a52d85f3-9510-5691-8cd4-aec1cbec2217" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437559} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3564637-e4ca-5adf-93e8-018d20d50745" :answer-field/key "At every point in the second quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438808} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c57c046d-8a73-5f59-9a49-b2aed7a95848" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437560} {:answer/id #uuid "70926e08-7c1e-54d5-abf7-3b2825ba6a80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437561}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70926e08-7c1e-54d5-abf7-3b2825ba6a80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437561} :answer-field/id #uuid "569f6144-ac61-57e2-9faa-b2202fc52224" :answer-field/key "At every point in the third quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438809} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81024236-c12a-5269-b2cd-e687e5de0a78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437562} {:answer/id #uuid "3d9cd6b7-f2cf-568e-a96b-b42a586b87cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437563}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d9cd6b7-f2cf-568e-a96b-b42a586b87cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437563} :answer-field/id #uuid "e68102cc-579c-585f-8f10-9a83a65a0a06" :answer-field/key "At every point in the fourth quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438810}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d92f1990-38b8-544d-a68e-c6daa48b1c4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-119766" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=6xy$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "First, we define $f(x,y)=6xy.$ Then, our differential equation is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).$ \n\nNotice that $f(x,y)=0$ when $x=0$ or $y=0.$ The lines $x=0$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into the usual four quadrants.\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each quadrant (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](images/6e/6ef852c9c15dc6f591119906abe479949f929edb6dc26f21588b4de545d02da2.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement “At every point in the second quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative” is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the second quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in the second quadrant.\n\n- Statement “At every point in the third quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive” is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the third quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in the third quadrant.\n\n- Statement “At every point in the fourth quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative” is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the fourth quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in the fourth quadrant.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “At every point in the second quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative”; “At every point in the third quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive”; “At every point in the fourth quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative”."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253597 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "caf9f9a0-fe1d-58a6-a9a8-b5a5e2a9514b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437564} {:answer/id #uuid "c6011e7b-933f-5512-8d31-3053e17fd0dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437565}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "caf9f9a0-fe1d-58a6-a9a8-b5a5e2a9514b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437564} :answer-field/id #uuid "ecff95c2-727a-5dae-ab02-1414391132f2" :answer-field/key "At every point in the first quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438811} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "791f7229-c5a9-5faf-8875-969a85713e63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437566} {:answer/id #uuid "a8d04d15-d334-5842-ab9f-31f5f6d0fe8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437567}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "791f7229-c5a9-5faf-8875-969a85713e63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437566} :answer-field/id #uuid "2fdf80c4-0c0f-5731-bd95-650894071c56" :answer-field/key "At every point in the second quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438812} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85f793cf-e920-5057-905e-6cd6b37d67be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437568} {:answer/id #uuid "42e66d50-d188-5d08-9d22-fe5a8a4b8fa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437569}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42e66d50-d188-5d08-9d22-fe5a8a4b8fa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437569} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3f69ac8-557d-5583-a34f-bae77d1add4a" :answer-field/key "At every point in the fourth quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438813} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1181fc42-b86d-50d6-8782-d9ed9b1e42c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186667888} {:answer/id #uuid "217ea4f1-ae91-53b2-b892-4e2f293b5b3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186667889}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1181fc42-b86d-50d6-8782-d9ed9b1e42c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186667888} :answer-field/id #uuid "df06b130-bf24-5498-a9e1-2e7063078c8c" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668221}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e15411d6-1039-50b8-8bc5-cef852747aab" :question/math-academy-id "q-119769" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-5xy$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "First, we define $f(x,y)=-5xy.$ Then, our differential equation is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).$ \n\nNotice that $f(x,y)=0$ when $x=0$ or $y=0.$ The lines $x=0$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into the usual four quadrants.\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each quadrant (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](images/2c/2c218c7df2bf4e159b4bffaccb4296beab8e3f44ab82d56b3589050af92ccf8e.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement “At every point in the first quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive” is false. If $(x,y)$ is in the first quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in the first quadrant.\n\n- Statement “At every point in the second quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative” is false. If $(x,y)$ is in the second quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in the second quadrant.\n\n- Statement “At every point in the fourth quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive” is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the fourth quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in the fourth quadrant.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “At every point in the fourth quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253601 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2acc8d45-b3cb-5472-808a-0ded96068ea4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437570} {:answer/id #uuid "5634aa7f-d685-513c-b71c-dd484269de1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437571}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2acc8d45-b3cb-5472-808a-0ded96068ea4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437570} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a1d4246-8605-5a10-a963-fb61cf7903ea" :answer-field/key "At every point in the first quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438814} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc34f3d5-600f-5ec4-ada6-979b186201d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437572} {:answer/id #uuid "a49a1c0b-79f3-538f-b749-b1d3bd1e40e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437573}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a49a1c0b-79f3-538f-b749-b1d3bd1e40e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437573} :answer-field/id #uuid "c41e5771-7f8b-5c44-9d14-38d54c1a1ee9" :answer-field/key "At every point in the third quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438815} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59bd4a0c-4bd0-55c9-ad16-d8c9e25d7727" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437574} {:answer/id #uuid "a8d6c719-9178-53d2-a8bc-b7c6358a8cc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437575}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59bd4a0c-4bd0-55c9-ad16-d8c9e25d7727" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437574} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a4fd295-2359-5c48-b9f5-fdc795da9a6a" :answer-field/key "At every point in the fourth quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438816} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cde11c7-cf2f-5cb9-95d2-dccab4335080" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186667890} {:answer/id #uuid "f2b3a6e2-a93b-59e6-854b-9492e52b5f82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186667891}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3cde11c7-cf2f-5cb9-95d2-dccab4335080" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186667890} :answer-field/id #uuid "2de75eeb-b32e-55a5-a13f-ac56c2af95a9" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668222}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c47e99b0-49b1-5362-97e1-164913960980" :question/math-academy-id "q-119774" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=8{x}^{2}{y}^{2}$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "First, we define $f(x,y)=8{x}^{2}{y}^{2}.$ Then, our differential equation is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).$ \n\nNotice that $f(x,y)=0$ when $x=0$ or $y=0.$ The lines $x=0$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into the usual four quadrants.\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each quadrant (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](images/d1/d1e4f48450a7f2032d755a5255d1a1e8a726dd16d273dae5dc1e587826d5ce58.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement “At every point in the first quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative” is false. If $(x,y)$ is in the first quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in the first quadrant.\n\n- Statement “At every point in the third quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive” is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the third quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in the third quadrant.\n\n- Statement “At every point in the fourth quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative” is false. If $(x,y)$ is in the fourth quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in the fourth quadrant.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “At every point in the third quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive” only."}]}
